Pre-Employment Medical Examination
General Practice Other
Request for occupational fitness assessment, Comprehensive physical/musculoskeletal exam, Urine drug/alcohol screening, Absence of acute complaints

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-030
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe anxiety and autonomic hyperarousal triggered by an immediate, mandatory occupational drug screen requirement threatening the patient's immediate livelihood.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a patient experiencing a severe panic attack regarding a mandatory, time-critical pre-employment medical and drug screen required by 0600 hours tomorrow for a FIFO (Fly-In Fly-Out) mining role. The patient was unable to secure an in-hours appointment and is highly distressed by the impending loss of a critical job opportunity. Urgent face-to-face assessment was necessary to de-escalate the acute psychiatric distress, physically rule out cardiovascular pathology secondary to the panic, and complete the mandatory clinical assessment and supervised urine screening to secure the patient's occupational status. Delaying assessment would have resulted in immediate termination of the contract and severe psychological decompensation.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "I need this medical and drug test signed off tonight or I lose my mining contract tomorrow morning. My heart is racing so fast I feel sick."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a formal request by a prospective employer to assess fitness for specific occupational duties (heavy machinery operation). The patient is experiencing severe, acute situational anxiety regarding the administrative deadline. A detailed review of the candidate's past medical history, surgical history, and current medications reveals no significant past medical history. The patient confirms an absolute absence of an acute medical presentation or specific patient-driven complaint, other than the severe anxiety generated by the deadline. The patient is terrified of failing the physical or the urine drug screen due to recent exposure to passive cannabis smoke, exacerbating the panic symptoms.
Functional Status: Fully independent, physically fit, but currently exhibiting high psychomotor agitation.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate threat to livelihood and financial stability; high situational distress.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 145/90 | HR: 105 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 88 Kg
General: Visibly anxious, sweating slightly, clutching the mandatory employer paperwork.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardic, regular rhythm, no murmurs. Blood pressure is mildly elevated secondary to anxiety. Respiratory: Clear air entry bilaterally. Musculoskeletal: A comprehensive physical examination demonstrates a normal, active range of motion, excellent lifting capacity, and intact joint stability. Neurological: Normal power, sensation, and reflexes. Assessment of visual acuity (Snellen 6/6 bilaterally) and colour vision (Ishihara normal) is completed. The clinical encounter mandates a urine drug and alcohol screening ensuring compliance with workplace safety policies. Expected investigation results include normal physiological parameters and a negative comprehensive urine drug screen fulfilling the employer's specific occupational health requirements.
Environmental Observations: Clean, well-lit environment suitable for a comprehensive physical examination; bathroom available for supervised urine collection.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications; denies any recent illicit substance use despite anxiety regarding passive exposure.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Pre-Employment Medical Examination (Complicated by Acute Situational Panic) - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Tachycardia and mild hypertension directly resolving upon the commencement of the physical exam and reassurance; absence of ischemic chest pain.

Illicit Substance Intoxication (e.g., Amphetamines) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal pupillary response, coherent speech, and an immediately subsequent negative point-of-care urine drug screen.

Generalized Anxiety Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The intense anxiety is entirely situational, acute, and immediately resolves once the medical clearance paperwork is successfully completed.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (High occupational importance)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient experienced a severe, acute panic attack strictly driven by a critical, imminent occupational deadline for a mandatory medical assessment. Telephone triage correctly identified that the patient's severe autonomic hyperarousal required medical evaluation. Urgent face-to-face assessment was strictly necessary to rule out a cardiac event caused by the extreme stress, provide immediate clinical reassurance, and meticulously complete the comprehensive physical examination and drug screening. By finalizing the paperwork on-site, the absolute trigger for the panic attack was neutralized, preventing an unwarranted ED presentation for acute anxiety and securing the patient's immediate employment. Deferring this assessment until the next in-hours period would have resulted in the loss of the contract and a massive psychological crisis.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Conducted a full cardiovascular, respiratory, and musculoskeletal examination. Performed visual acuity and colour vision testing. Conducted a supervised, point-of-care 6-panel urine drug screen (resulting negative for all substances, including THC). Completed and signed the formal employer medical documentation. Provided robust verbal reassurance regarding the negative drug screen, resulting in immediate resolution of the tachycardia.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent this severe level of administrative anxiety in the future, commit to utilizing a digital "document vault" app on your smartphone tonight. Scan and upload this completed medical certificate immediately. By physically securing a digital backup of all your critical occupational clearances, you actively remove the fear of lost paperwork and build a structured, highly reliable system for your demanding FIFO lifestyle.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: No clinical follow-up required; patient is medically cleared for duty.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 immediately if you experience sudden, severe chest pain, sudden shortness of breath, or if you feel completely faint and dizzy upon standing, regardless of your medical clearance.

